## Pareto frontier -- the defensible choices, not a single ordering's winner

A card is on the frontier when no other ranked card is at least as good on all of (median, worst-window, hit-rate) *and* strictly better on at least one. This is the honest answer to "which cards are defensible choices" -- a single ordering, whichever criterion it uses, cannot give that answer on its own.

**Exposure caveat.** The worst-window criterion structurally favours low-exposure books -- a book that never puts much capital at risk cannot sustain a deep drawdown, so a small book reaches a shallow worst window almost for free, the same way a large negative benchmark structurally favours it on adjusted_excess (see the incommensurable-exposure flag above). Frontier membership earned mainly on the worst-window axis is not the same finding as frontier membership earned on the median, and both are weaker evidence for a card whose `mean_exposure` sits well below the field's -- read the column below beside every row rather than trusting frontier membership alone.

